
<% content_for :title, "Books | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="perspectives-books-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="perspectives-books-hero">

    <div class="perspectives-books-hero-content">

      <p class="perspectives-books-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / PERSPECTIVES / BOOKS
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BETWEEN</span>
        <span>TWO</span>
        <span>COVERS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="perspectives-books-intro">
        OTHER PEOPLE'S STORIES.<br>
        OTHER WAYS OF THINKING.<br>
        WHAT YOU FOUND BETWEEN THE PAGES.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- INTRO -->
  <section class="pb-intro-section">

    <p class="pb-number">01 / THE BOOKSHELF</p>

    <h2>
      I DON'T WANT<br>
      A FUCKING<br>
      READING LIST.
    </h2>

    <div class="pb-copy">

      <p>
        You've read plenty of books that offered
        advice, explanations, philosophies, or
        completely different ways of looking
        at the world. Some stayed with you.
        Others made you question what you
        thought you knew.
      </p>

      <p>
        This isn't about collecting books
        you're supposed to read or agreeing
        with everything an author says.
        It's about remembering what you
        actually took from them.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some books help you understand suffering.
        Others make you think about addiction,
        responsibility, identity, or the
        consequences of your decisions.
        Some have nothing to do with recovery
        and still manage to tell you
        something about your own life.
      </p>


      <p>
        What matters isn't whether the book
        was universally brilliant or whether
        anyone else would recommend it.
        It's what made you stop reading,
        reconsider something, or recognize
        an experience you hadn't quite
        found the words for.
      </p>

      <div class="pb-callout">
        THE QUESTION ISN'T<br>
        "WOULD SOMEONE ELSE READ THIS?"<br><br>
        IT'S "WHAT DID YOU TAKE FROM IT, <br>
	AND WHY DID YOU LIKE THIS BOOK?"
      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- BOOKS -->
  <section class="pb-library">

    <div class="pb-library-heading">

      <p>02 / ON YOUR SHELF</p>

      <h2>
        READ.<br>
        THINK.<br>
        `ARGUE` WITH IT.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="pb-books-grid">


      <!-- THICH NHAT HANH -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-pink">

        <p class="pb-book-number">01</p>

        <p class="pb-book-type">
          BUDDHISM / MINDFULNESS
        </p>

        <h3>
          THE HEART OF THE<br>
          BUDDHA'S TEACHING
        </h3>

        <p class="pb-author">
          THICH NHAT HANH
        </p>

        <div class="pb-book-copy">

          <p>
            You return to Buddhism because it
            gives you a way to understand
            suffering without turning every
            difficult experience into something
            you need to eliminate immediately.
		It's got instructions for life that
		are easy to follow (although are hard to practice).
		It's got a way to follow that shows you
		a way to peace.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thich Nhat Hanh asks you to pay
            attention to what you're experiencing,
            recognize craving and attachment,
            and approach yourself with compassion
            rather than constant judgment.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't have to become perfectly
            mindful or peaceful to find
            something useful in his teachings.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div class="pb-takeaway">
          <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
          <p>
            You can acknowledge suffering
            without immediately trying
            to escape it. <br>
		The Eightfold Path is the way
		to managing life and happiness.
          </p>
        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- DHAMMAPADA -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-yellow">

        <p class="pb-book-number">02</p>

        <p class="pb-book-type">
          BUDDHISM / WISDOM
        </p>

        <h3>
          THE<br>
          DHAMMAPADA
        </h3>

        <p class="pb-author">
          BUDDHIST TEXT
        </p>

        <div class="pb-book-copy">

          <p>
            You can read a few lines of
            the Dhammapada and find yourself
            thinking about them for the
            rest of the afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its teachings about the mind,
            anger, craving, attention, and
            conduct are particularly relevant
            when you consider how often
            you've acted on something
            simply because you felt it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A thought can appear without
            requiring your participation.
            That's easier to understand
            intellectually than to remember
            when you're pissed off.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div class="pb-takeaway">
          <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
          <p>
            Just because your mind produces
            a thought doesn't mean you
            have to follow the thing.
          </p>
        </div>

      </article>


      
<!-- CARRIE FISHER -->
<article class="pb-book pb-book-purple">

  <p class="pb-book-number">03</p>

  <p class="pb-book-type">
    MEMOIR / BIPOLAR / ADDICTION / HUMOR
  </p>

  <h3>
    WISHFUL<br>
    DRINKING
  </h3>

  <p class="pb-author">
    CARRIE FISHER
  </p>

  <div class="pb-book-copy">

    <p>
      Carrie Fisher manages to talk about
      bipolar disorder, addiction, psychiatric
      treatment, family dysfunction, and
      some spectacularly unfortunate decisions
      without making the whole thing
      unbearably solemn.
    </p>

    <p>
      There's something refreshing about
      someone discussing experiences you
      recognize without demanding that
      every difficult moment become
      an inspirational lesson.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can take your mental health
      seriously and still find humor
      in the absurdity of living
      with it. The two aren't
      mutually exclusive.
    </p>

    <p>
      And you don't have to make
      your entire identity revolve
      around your diagnoses
      or your mistakes.
    </p>

  </div>

  <div class="pb-takeaway">
    <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
    <p>
      You can acknowledge that
      your life has been a
      ridiculous fucking mess
      at times without losing
      your sense of humor
      or reducing yourself
      to your worst experiences.
    </p>
  </div>

</article>



      <!-- FOUR AGREEMENTS -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-black">

        <p class="pb-book-number">04</p>

        <p class="pb-book-type">
          PERSONAL GROWTH
        </p>

        <h3>
          THE FOUR<br>
          AGREEMENTS
        </h3>

        <p class="pb-author">
          DON MIGUEL RUIZ
        </p>

        <div class="pb-book-copy">

          <p>
            Four straightforward ideas
            that become considerably
            less straightforward when
            you actually try to follow them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Especially when you're angry,
            convinced you know what someone
            meant, or already halfway
            through an argument that
            hasn't actually happened.
          </p>

          <div class="pb-mini-list">
            <span>BE IMPECCABLE WITH YOUR WORD.</span>
            <span>DON'T TAKE THINGS PERSONALLY.</span>
            <span>DON'T MAKE ASSUMPTIONS.</span>
            <span>ALWAYS DO YOUR BEST.</span>
          </div>

          <p>
            You can understand all four
            agreements and still find
            yourself doing the exact
            opposite on a difficult day.
          </p>

          <p>
            That's a reason to revisit
            them, not another opportunity
            to criticize yourself.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div class="pb-takeaway">
          <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
          <p>
            You create a remarkable amount
            of unnecessary frustration when
            you decide you already know
            what someone else is thinking.
          </p>
        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- GUCCI MANE -->
      <article class="pb-book pb-book-yellow">

        <p class="pb-book-number">05</p>

        <p class="pb-book-type">
          MEMOIR / ADDICTION
        </p>

        <h3>
          THE AUTOBIOGRAPHY<br>
          OF GUCCI MANE
        </h3>

        <p class="pb-author">
          GUCCI MANE
        </p>

        <div class="pb-book-copy">

          <p>
            Addiction, jail, consequences,
            ambition, identity, and the
            complicated business of
            rebuilding a life.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need another person
            to have lived your exact
            circumstances to recognize
            something familiar in
            their story.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can appreciate an account
            of someone's mistakes and
            changes without turning
            them into a role model
            or excusing everything
            they've done.
          </p>

          <p>
            And not every useful book
            about recovery needs
            <em>recovery</em> written
            across the cover.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div class="pb-takeaway">
          <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
          <p>
            Your past explains some
            of how you got here.
            It doesn't get permanent
            ownership of what
            you do next.
          </p>
        </div>

      </article>


<!-- ICEBERG SLIM -->
<article class="pb-book pb-book-pink">

  <p class="pb-book-number">06</p>

  <p class="pb-book-type">
    MEMOIR / POWER / MANIPULATION / SURVIVAL
  </p>

  <h3>
    PIMP:<br>
    THE STORY<br>
    OF MY LIFE
  </h3>

  <p class="pb-author">
    ICEBERG SLIM
  </p>

  <div class="pb-book-copy">

    <p>
      This isn't a comfortable book,
      and it isn't supposed to be.
      Iceberg Slim describes a world
      of exploitation, violence,
      manipulation, and people
      using one another.
    </p>

    <p>
      What's interesting is how
      someone learns to rationalize
      behavior that causes enormous
      harm, particularly when that
      behavior becomes part of
      their identity and livelihood.
    </p>

    <p>
      You don't have to admire
      the narrator or excuse
      his actions to examine
      how power, desperation,
      self-interest, and
      justification work.
    </p>

    <p>
      It's worth considering how
      easily people construct
      explanations for what
      they're doing, especially
      when admitting the truth
      would require them
      to change.
    </p>

  </div>

  <div class="pb-takeaway">
    <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
    <p>
      Being able to explain
      your behavior doesn't
      automatically justify it.
      Understanding why you
      did something is only
      part of taking
      responsibility for it.
    </p>
  </div>

</article>


<!-- MATTHEW MCCONAUGHEY -->
<article class="pb-book pb-book-purple">

  <p class="pb-book-number">07</p>

  <p class="pb-book-type">
    MEMOIR / PHILOSOPHY / PERSPECTIVE
  </p>

  <h3>
    GREENLIGHTS
  </h3>

  <p class="pb-author">
    MATTHEW MCCONAUGHEY
  </p>

  <div class="pb-book-copy">

    <p>
      Matthew McConaughey turns decades
      of journals, stories, successes,
      mistakes, and bizarre experiences
      into a rather unconventional
      philosophy about life.
    </p>

    <p>
      The interesting part isn't
      the idea that everything
      happens for a reason.
      You don't have to believe
      that every disaster is
      secretly a blessing.
    </p>

    <p>
      It's the possibility that
      an experience can change
      meaning depending on how
      you understand it later.
    </p>

    <p>
      You've had circumstances
      you couldn't control and
      consequences you absolutely
      could have avoided. Both
      have influenced where
      you are now.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can't rewrite what
      happened, but you can
      reconsider what you
      take from it.
    </p>

  </div>

  <div class="pb-takeaway">
    <span>WHAT STUCK</span>
    <p>
      You don't have to be
      grateful for every shitty
      experience to recognize
      that it changed the way
      you see things.
    </p>
  </div>

</article>

      
<!-- ANTHONY BOURDAIN -->
<article class="pb-book pb-book-yellow">

  <p class="pb-book-number">08</p>

  <p class="pb-book-type">
    MEMOIR / ADDICTION / FOOD / CHAOS
  </p>

  <h3>
    KITCHEN<br>
    CONFIDENTIAL
  </h3>

  <p class="pb-author">
    ANTHONY BOURDAIN
  </p>

  <div class="pb-book-copy">

    <p>
      Anthony Bourdain takes you into
      professional kitchens filled with
      enormous personalities, questionable
      decisions, relentless work, drugs,
      alcohol, and a remarkable amount
      of profanity.
    </p>

    <p>
      What makes it interesting isn't
      simply the chaos. It's the way
      Bourdain writes about a world
      he genuinely loved without
      pretending that everything
      happening inside it was
      particularly healthy.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can recognize the appeal
      of excess, the camaraderie,
      the excitement, and the feeling
      of belonging somewhere that
      operates by its own rules.
    </p>

    <p>
      But loving an experience
      doesn't mean you have to
      defend every part of it.
      Sometimes the things that
      make a lifestyle exciting
      are also the things
      that make it destructive.
    </p>

    <p>
      You don't have to romanticize
      the damage to appreciate
      the stories, the humor,
      or the passion behind them.
    </p>

  </div>

  <div class="pb-takeaway">
    <span>WHAT STUCK</span>

    <p>
      You can love parts of a
      chaotic life without wanting
      to live that way anymore.
      Enjoying the memories doesn't
      obligate you to repeat them.
    </p>

  </div>

</article>


<!-- OPEN SLOT -->
<article class="pb-book pb-book-empty">

  <p class="pb-book-number">09</p>

  <h3>
    WHAT'S<br>
    NEXT?
  </h3>

  <p>
    Your shelf isn't
    fucking finished.
  </p>

</article>


    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- HOW YOU READ -->
  <section class="pb-reading-section">

    <p class="pb-number">03 / HOW YOU READ</p>

    <h2>
      KEEP WHAT<br>
      MAKES YOU<br>
      THINK.
    </h2>

    <div class="pb-copy">

      <p>
        You don't need to agree with
        an entire book for one idea
        inside it to matter.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can underline a paragraph
        and disagree with the very
        next page. You can appreciate
        a writer's perspective without
        making that person an authority
        over your entire fucking life.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes a book gives you
        language for something you've
        experienced. Sometimes it
        challenges an assumption.
        Sometimes it just makes
        you realize how differently
        another person sees the world.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't have to turn
        everything you read into
        a life lesson, either.
        You can enjoy a story
        because it's interesting,
        funny, disturbing, strange,
        or beautifully written.
      </p>

      <p>
        But when something stays
        with you, it's worth
        figuring out why.
      </p>

      <div class="pb-reading-grid">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>What made you stop reading and think?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>What did you disagree with?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>What connected to something you've lived through?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>What idea followed you after you closed the book?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>05</span>
          <p>Did anything actually change how you behave?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>06</span>
          <p>What do you want to come back to later?</p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="pb-ending">

    <p>
      READ THE BOOK.<br>
      QUESTION THE BOOK.<br>
      WRITE IN THE MARGINS.
    </p>

    <h2>
      THINK FOR<br>
      YOURSELF.
    </h2>

    <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
        perspectives_path,
        class: "pb-back-link" %>

  </section>

</main>
 